  "image": "sha256:d6ffd842978849651e756ebd39e9bf2da7ddb03e746113013533029878485a24 (spark-ds41f:candidate-48), round-1 core files bind-mounted read-only; no build",
  "mark": "0 LRU inversions (retention 0/512/8192/None), stress-probe hit share >= 1107 on tp3 (retention 512) and tp4 (retention 8192), boundary matrix 1404/1404, spec-decode 60/60, the PR's 23-file test list passing; both settings, two boots",
  "verdict": "no-defensible-correction",
  "reason": "No design meets the whole mark, and the inversion definition is not wrong, so I did not change it. (1) The definition measures the PR's stated purpose. The PR's test says 'partial eviction from the BlockPool always evicts the newest tail blocks first and preserves the root prefix blocks'. Its README places 'root checkpoints (512, 8,192) at the MRU tail (evicted last)'. Its LRU scenario is built to catch turn 1's skipped SWA checkpoints, which are blocks outside the finished request's run. Counting only within the run would drop that stated target: round 1 then scores 0. Counting per KV group still gives round 1 6, all of them in the SWA group. The check also guards a real property. With round 1's order, the extension hit stays at 25,088 while the 52 aged checkpoints are evicted; on the 53rd eviction it falls to 0, with every MLA block still cached. Under the monotone order it falls in steps of 256 or 512 tokens, one step every 3 to 5 evicted blocks. (2) Bound. With 0 inversions, each conversation's cached blocks leave the free queue in non-increasing position order, MLA before SWA at a tie. Then the first cached block in the queue is some conversation's top, and that block is on the conversation's next hit path. Each block the stress eviction takes is therefore either one of the at most 3 non-redundant cached blocks at a lost 256-token position of a live conversation, or a redundant copy. A redundant copy exists only for a position that was lost earlier and re-prefilled, and a lost position yields at most 3 of these, counting its own evicted blocks. The three evictions take 54 cached blocks, so at least 18 positions (4,608 tokens) are lost, and the hit is at most 1,121,792 (99.4489%). 1107 is 2,048 tokens below the ceiling on tp3 and 3,584 on tp4, which is 8 and 14 positions. Measured: at most 3 blocks per position at every eviction. The best 0-inversion order the harness found, choosing each eviction's split between the two conversations by the real lookup: tp3 1,121,536 (99.4262%, -2,816 vs 1107), tp4 1,120,512 (99.3355%, -2,304). With dense SWA retention it reaches 1,121,792, the bound itself. (3) The step from the LRU scenario to the stress probe assumes the queue rule is the same for both. The LRU scenario runs retention 512 and 8192 through the same cache_blocks, remove_skipped_blocks and free paths as the stress profiles. Their only differences are the number of conversations, chunked prefill and the pool size. A rule keyed to those would game the probe; it would not fix the code. (4) The fewest inversions compatible with the stress clause is 1: aged checkpoints must precede some live top. The floor1 ordering reaches 1/1/1/1 at the ceiling, 99.8574% on both profiles; round 1 has 6. The two existing corrections each miss exactly one clause. The original 0763 (candidate-48) meets 0 inversions but its stress share is -3,328 / -6,400 tokens below 1107. Round 1 is at the ceiling but has 6 inversions. At 36 blocks per eviction, the count the PR's own test comment names, round 1 collapses on tp4 to 93.19% (agent 0's turn 4 hits 0), as does the 1107 reconstruction (92.89%); the best 0-inversion order keeps 98.65%. Choosing between protecting the extension and degrading gracefully is a decision about the mark, not a code fix. Round 2 delivers no patch.",
  "patch": null,
  "patch_note": "none delivered. Round 1's patch (rework/pr-1010-r1/0763-...patch, sha256 446274bc66b5e38575e0e99f7f47dc9bedec76409df7750ae8d90e6466765568) remains the best correction for the stress clause; the original 0763 in candidate-48 is the one that meets the inversion clause. No code change I could make moves either past the bound.",
